# Symbols whose type in the containers of the previous build is wrong
# (see the README).  In kernel modules the CTF
# step ran before the EXPORT_SYMS/PREFIX_SYMS symbol localization, which
# reorders .symtab; CTF refers to symbols by table position, so a group
# of symbols ended up with each other's types.  ctfmerge was right when
# it ran, and cannot run later (it leaves localized symbols untyped), so
# the per-object path keeps this; the post-link path converts after the
# rewrite and is not affected.  These entries describe the old
# containers.
#
# Two kinds of line:
#   module rotation      ctfcmp.py may apply its rotation rule to this
#                        module (symbols whose old type is another
#                        differing symbol's new type and vice versa).
#                        The rule is symmetric and cannot tell a stale
#                        old container from a permutation the new tool
#                        would introduce, so it is restricted to the
#                        modules where the error was verified against
#                        the source; anywhere else a rotation is an
#                        unclassified SYMBOL line.
#   module kind name     this one symbol (kind is obj or func), for the
#                        cases the rule does not reach.
# The module is the basename of the old dump up to its first dot; the
# kernel is "kernel".
agp rotation
pseudofs rotation
lindebugfs rotation
mqueuefs rotation
libiconv rotation
cd9660 rotation
msdosfs rotation
procfs rotation
udf rotation
# the kernel links cd9660, msdosfs, agp, procfs and pseudofs
kernel rotation
# The entries below are name-matching errors of the old merger, not
# stale positions: if_cxgb localizes no symbols, and the userland symbol
# table is never rewritten.
# if_cxgb: static int nfilters (cxgb_main.c) got the type of a parameter
# of that name in cxgb_mc5.c.
if_cxgb obj nfilters
# libc: the FBSD_1.0 compat symbol timezone is an alias of
# __timezone_compat(int, int) returning char * (gen/timezone.c,
# __sym_compat); new types it by address, old typed it by name as
# long (void), which nothing in libc defines.
libc func timezone
# ifconfig: four files define a static ``params'' (iflagg.c, ifvlan.c,
# ifvxlan.c, ifieee80211.c); for the ifieee80211.c one old took the type
# of a function-local ``uint32_t params'' of the same file (line 2837
# vs the file-level struct ieee80211_clone_params at 6077).  New types
# it by address.
ifconfig obj params
